title: 5. The readers the tier names read it, Tay before Libby, in one mission each and one round; each writes only what to change.
pid: e2eeb71a-52ba-4cf0-a683-a0d523c6da2f
body: |2-
  Tay goes first, because if the request is not worth doing Libby's
  reading is wasted. Tay answers: is it worth it; how far should it
  go, against the appetite; and which questions for Jeff his taste
  already answers. Libby answers: could a mind holding only the graph
  carry out each building mission from it, and could someone who
  finds it later tell why each decision was made.
  Each brief says: report only what would change what gets built, or
  whether it can be followed; skip style. A reviewer told to find
  problems always finds some, and fixing every one bloats the work.
  Each writes a short note in its own graph, a headline per change,
  most important first, as Tay did on 2026-11-12 in
  [[id:d7252567-aa4b-40b4-abf5-7dd7053a8d4e][Claude's design for consuming another's changes: drop the count, keep the baseline and the marks, and ask Jeff one question of eight.]]
  That one reading turned eight questions for Jeff into one.
  There is no second round. The first building mission folds the
  readings into the plan before it builds. If Tay says not to do it,
  the plan becomes 'abandoned'; if Tay cuts it to a different
  request, it goes back to its author.
  Rests on: read (Claude Code's best practices: a fresh reviewer that
  never saw the reasoning, and "skip style"; Nygard: a record is a
  conversation with a future developer). Ours: one round, the order,
  the builder folding in (unsure).
